<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    canPerformGestures is the whole mechanism: the first spike established that the accessibility SELECTION
    actions stop at the same editable-buffer boundary InputConnection does, and the gesture spike that
    dispatchGesture does not, because it drives the target app's own selection UI instead.

    flagRetrieveInteractiveWindows is needed for the floating-toolbar window, which is what locates
    a selection whose node bounds do not hug its text.

    flagRequestAccessibilityButton must NEVER be here (a regression found on a OnePlus 15,
    2026-11-04; ServiceConfigTest guards it). For a service targeting API 30+ that requests the
    button, Android makes the service "always-on" (AccessibilityFragmentType.INVISIBLE_TOGGLE): it is
    enabled exactly while it has a shortcut. ShortcutUtils.updateInvisibleToggleAccessibilityServiceEnableState
    turns the service OFF when its last shortcut goes, and on the phone the floating button showed
    with no shortcut picked by the user. So the button was forced on, and dragging it away
    switched the whole service off. That page type also has no switch, so Settings could not turn it back on.
    setServiceInfo cannot set the flag at runtime either (REQUEST_ACCESSIBILITY_BUTTON_CHANGE), so
    the system button cannot toggle the pad at all; the tile and the notification do that.

    Deliberately NOT isAccessibilityTool: this is the ordinary Play lane, per the policy reading in
    the gesture spike's notes. Claiming the flag would be arguable and risky; the ordinary lane is well trodden.
-->
<accessibility-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:accessibilityEventTypes="typeViewTextSelectionChanged|typeViewScrolled|typeWindowContentChanged|typeWindowStateChanged|typeWindowsChanged"
    android:accessibilityFeedbackType="feedbackGeneric"
    android:accessibilityFlags="flagDefault|flagIncludeNotImportantViews|flagRetrieveInteractiveWindows"
    android:canRetrieveWindowContent="true"
    android:canPerformGestures="true"
    android:description="@string/accessibility_service_description"
    android:notificationTimeout="0" />
